---
// What to bring and what happens on test day (docs/seo-sprint.md: "what to bring to citizenship test",
// "what to expect"). Booking and rescheduling live on /australia/book-citizenship-test/. Every fact is quoted from
// docs/facts.md. Answers are written once and used for both the page and its FAQPage structured data.
import Guide from '../../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { url } from '../../../lib/url';
import { SOURCES, TEST, YEAR } from '../../../data/au/facts';
import { GUIDES, PRACTICE } from '../../../data/au/guides';

const qa = {
  bring: {
    q: 'What do I need to bring to the citizenship test?',
    a: 'Photo ID, such as an Australian driver licence, a passport or an Australian issued proof of age card, and your appointment letter, printed or on your phone. Also bring any other documents Home Affairs asked you to bring.',
  },
  noId: {
    q: 'What if I forget my photo ID?',
    a: 'Home Affairs says: “If you do not bring a photo ID, your appointment will be changed to a later date.” It also says: “We will not accept certified copies or electronic images of your photo ID.”',
  },
  children: {
    q: 'Can I bring my children to the test?',
    a: 'No. Home Affairs says children are not permitted in the test centre and that there are no childcare facilities, so arrange care before you go.',
  },
  help: {
    q: 'Can someone help me during the test?',
    a: 'You must sit the test on your own and can’t bring anyone to help. A Test Facilitator can show you how to use the computer or tablet or use it for you, give you headphones, read the questions aloud, and select your answers if you say them.',
  },
  result: {
    q: 'When do I get my citizenship test result?',
    a: 'Straight away. Home Affairs says: “Your result will be displayed on the screen after you complete the test.”',
  },
};
const faq = Object.values(qa).map(({ q, a }) => ({ q, a }));
---
<Guide
  title={`What to Bring to the Australian Citizenship Test (${YEAR})`}
  description="Photo ID and your appointment letter, what ID counts, what happens if you forget it, and what happens at the centre. Quoted from Home Affairs."
  path="australia/test-day/"
  h1="What to bring to the citizenship test, and what happens on the day"
  answer="Bring photo ID (such as a passport or an Australian driver licence) and your appointment letter, printed or on your phone. Arrive 10 minutes early. Home Affairs confirms your identity, then you sit the test on your own on a computer or tablet, and your result appears on the screen."
  sources={['test']}
  related={[GUIDES.book, GUIDES.format, PRACTICE]}
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2 id="what-to-bring">{qa.bring.q}</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Photo ID.</strong> <a href={SOURCES.test}>Home Affairs</a> gives these examples: an Australian driver licence, a passport, or an Australian issued proof of age card.</li>
    <li><strong>Your appointment letter</strong>, “either as a printed hard copy or displayed on your phone”.</li>
    <li>Any other documents Home Affairs asked you to bring.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before the day, “ensure that you have attached all documents to your application in ImmiAccount”. If something has changed since you applied, such as your address or your name, attach your new documents there too.</p>

  <h2 id="forgot-id">{qa.noId.q}</h2>
  <blockquote><p>“If you do not bring a photo ID, your appointment will be changed to a later date.”</p><p>“We will not accept certified copies or electronic images of your photo ID.”</p><cite>Home Affairs, Learn about the citizenship test</cite></blockquote>
  <p>So a photo of your passport on your phone isn’t enough: bring the ID itself.</p>

  <h2 id="at-the-centre">What happens at the citizenship test appointment?</h2>
  <p>“Arrive 10 minutes before your appointment time.” At your appointment Home Affairs will discuss your citizenship application, confirm your identity and take your photo. You sit the test after your identity is confirmed.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>You must not bring study materials into the testing area. Staff tell you where to put your belongings.</li>
    <li>You use a computer or tablet, and you have {TEST.minutes} minutes for {TEST.questions} questions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="children">{qa.children.q}</h2>
  <p>{qa.children.a}</p>

  <h2 id="the-test">{qa.help.q}</h2>
  <p>“You must sit the test on your own. You cannot bring anyone with you to help you during the test.” A Test Facilitator can support you by:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>showing you how to use the computer or tablet, or using it for you,</li>
    <li>giving you headphones to listen to the questions,</li>
    <li>reading the test questions aloud,</li>
    <li>selecting your answers if you say them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some people can sit an <a href={url(GUIDES.who.path) + '#assisted-test'}>Assisted Test</a> with 90 minutes. You need to ask for it in your application.</p>

  <h2 id="rules">What are the rules during the test?</h2>
  <p>During the test you must not:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use mobile phones or other electronic devices, such as laptops, cameras, smart watches or smart glasses,</li>
    <li>talk to other people, other than the Test Facilitator,</li>
    <li>use books, notes or any other materials,</li>
    <li>copy answers from another person.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You must also finish within the time: {TEST.minutes} minutes for the standard test, 90 minutes for the Assisted Test. Home Affairs warns: “If you do not follow the rules, we may decide that you have not successfully completed the test and refuse your application.”</p>

  <h2 id="after">{qa.result.q}</h2>
  <p>{qa.result.a} Home Affairs keeps processing your application and writes to you if it needs more information or when it makes a decision.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>If you pass</strong> and your application is approved, you must attend a citizenship ceremony. Home Affairs sends an invitation letter “about four weeks before the ceremony”, and “Wait times for citizenship ceremonies can vary.”</li>
    <li><strong>If you don’t pass</strong>, your visa is not affected and Home Affairs books another appointment at no extra cost. See <a href={url(GUIDES.failed.path)}>how many attempts you get</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="appointment-letter">Your appointment letter</h2>
  <p>After you apply, Home Affairs sends you a letter with the date, time and place of your appointment. To change it, follow the instructions in the letter. See <a href={url(GUIDES.book.path)}>how booking and rescheduling work</a>.</p>
</Guide>
